  I've got bad news, good news, and indifferent news.  The bad
news is that some of my statements on effects of the spells of
magic missile, cone of cold, and fireball at the various skill
levels have been inaccurate.


My current misunderstanding of damage vs skill vs XL:

  Magic Missile and Cone of cold use the [XL/2 +1] d6 as basic
spell damage at all levels of skill.  Fireball damage does not 
depend on XL at any level of skill.  

  That last despite the use of the phrase "spell damage" in the 
description of the damage done by the Fireball spell at skilled+ 
skill level.  The spoiler specifically defines "spell damage"
to mean [XL/2 +1] on about line 10.  

  I suggest the term "spell damage" to refer to the value [XL/2 +1] 
should be replaced with the term "damage factor", just as the
spoiler replaces use of the term "spell bonus" with "damage
bonus" almost immediately in the text.



  The good news is that the average damage estimates for a d6 
have been *low* for all spells.  The average result of the roll 
of a six-sided die (aka: d6) is 3.5, not 3.0.



  I've double-checked the following.  The indifferent news is
that my previous statements had also been double-checked.
So I'm including info copied from the spell spoiler.


  The excerpts from the spell spoiler are not complete for each
spell.  Generally, just the damage sections are complete.  


Notes on terminology:

  Note that the phrase "spell damage" may be used inconsistently
in the spell spoiler, sometimes meaning the value referred to 
above as <damage factor>, sometimes possibly meaning the actual 
damage done by the spell.

  'spell damage' and 'spell bonus' are denoted with single
quotes the first time they are used.  They are never so denoted
again.  Indeed, 'spell bonus' is never used again.  'Damage
bonus' (undenoted) is apparently used instead.




  Lines from the spoiler are preceded by ">", and some have
been slightly modified for ease of viewing.


